  "hints": [
    "Choose line breaks before deciding how many spaces each gap gets.",
    "Greedily take the longest word prefix that fits with one required space per gap.",
    "For a nonfinal line with k>1 words, divmod(width-letter_count, k-1) gives the base gap and number of leftmost gaps receiving one extra space.",
    "Treat the last line and single-word lines separately: join with single spaces and pad only on the right. Spaces are part of the exact answer."
  ],
  "editorial": "First scan to the maximal fitting word group, counting letters and the minimum gaps. On ordinary multiword lines, divide the available space budget across the gaps and allocate the remainder from left to right. Last lines and single-word lines are left-justified. The reference is linear in the total emitted characters plus input traversal; output padding must be counted in that cost. The independent oracle enumerates every legal break pattern on small inputs, chooses the lexicographically greatest sequence of per-line word counts, and inserts spaces round-robin. The workbook example lost repeated spaces; its original text and explicit correction are retained in the validation report.",
